\details{
This is what \code{\link[=catchAtAoLAggregation]{catchAtAoLAggregation()}} (the numbers) and
\code{\link[=meanWoLatAoLAggregation]{meanWoLatAoLAggregation()}} (the mean weights) add up - they are given the
same arguments here, and add up the same way - in the long format of the
SCF instead of one column per class: a \code{Number} row (\code{valueType} \code{Total}) and
a \code{WeightLive} row (\code{valueType} \code{Mean}) for each class. A class no fish
fall in is left out, since it has no mean weight.

The distributions are linked to the catch through \code{domainBiology}, the catch
category and the country, and the \code{grouping} is read off the catch they are
linked to. A sampled distribution has the domain of its catch. The strata an
allocation estimated a distribution for have none in the raised catch, and the
allocation made one up for the distribution and for the \code{catch} it returns,
\code{"[alloc]_<season>_<area>_<unit>_<fleet>_<category>"}; a catch row with no
domain is given the same, so every stratum - raised or not, sampled or
allocated - finds its distribution whether the raised catch the allocation was
run on or the \code{catch} of its result is handed in
(\code{distributionsCombined(alloc$distribution, alloc$catch, ...)}). It has to be
that raised catch: another raising has other strata and weights. A catch weight
with no distribution, or a distribution with fish and no catch weight, is
warned about. Take one \code{originType_catch} and one \code{variableType_catch} too, as
in \code{\link[=catchAtAoLAggregation]{catchAtAoLAggregation()}}.

The \code{grouping} is the one of \code{\link[=catchCombined]{catchCombined()}} (and of the same file: a
distribution needs a catch of the same year, stock, category, season, area and
fleet in the CC), with the sex of the distribution on top -
\code{attributeType} and \code{attributeValue}, the default - which is \code{NA} when the
distributions do not have one. The header - \code{workingGroupYear} and the rest -
is the one of \code{\link[=catchCombined]{catchCombined()}} too, and so is the sampling's reading.
\subsection{The sampling}{

\code{PSU}, \code{numPSUs}, \code{numTrips} and \code{numMeasurements} say how much sampling
stands behind each class. They are taken from the columns of the
distributions with the same names, as the CEF has them. A row of the table is
made of the distributions of the catch it is added up from: each counts once,
however many classes it has (the CEF repeats its counts on every class, and a
plus group puts several of them in one row), and the counts of the
distributions are summed. They are \code{NA} when none of the distributions reports
one - the allocation leaves them empty for the distributions it estimated,
which were borrowed: the format asks for the strata that actually contributed
\itemize{
\item and \code{PSU}, the type of the sampling unit, is that of the distributions that
report a number of them, a \verb{;} separated list when there are several types.
\code{numMeasurements} is the count of the whole distribution, which is all the CEF
has, though the format asks for the measurements at the class: do not sum it
over the classes. \code{variance} is left empty.
}
}

